# Backstop for a LEGACY (non-BuildKit) `docker build`. `Dockerfile.dockerignore` next to this file
# is the ignore-file that actually governs a real build: BuildKit's per-Dockerfile convention takes
# FULL precedence over this one and is what `docker/build-push-action` (CI, `publish-image.yml`)
# and a modern `docker build` (BuildKit is the default builder since Docker 23, per this repo's own
# `Dockerfile` header) both read. This file only matters if BuildKit is somehow off
# (`DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0`, or a pre-23 Docker) — a plain legacy `docker build` has no notion that
# `Dockerfile.dockerignore` exists at all and falls back to this file alone, read from the context
# root exactly the way any other `.dockerignore` is.
#
# Deliberately short: only the patterns whose absence would leak a secret or a developer's live
# database into an image. `Dockerfile.dockerignore` carries the full exclusion list (build output,
# node_modules, test artifacts, ...) with the fuller reasoning for each; this file mirrors only its
# security-relevant subset so there's one obvious place to keep both in sync, not two independent
# lists that can quietly drift apart.

.env
.env.*
!.env.example

.certs
.certs.disabled
**/*.pem
**/*.key

sites/*/content.db
sites/*/content.db-wal
sites/*/content.db-shm
sites/*/content.db.bak-*
sites/*/restore-point-*.db
chat.db
chat.db-wal
chat.db-shm
chat.db.bak-*
content.db
content.db-wal
content.db-shm
content.db.bak-*
apps/website/sites

.git
.claude

# Host-built dependencies (macOS/arm64 binaries for better-sqlite3, argon2, sharp — same reason
# `Dockerfile.dockerignore` excludes these). Not a secret, but a legacy builder with no exclusion
# at all would otherwise bake a contributor's entire host `node_modules` into the image.
node_modules
apps/*/node_modules
